# 8. PHASE 6: LOW-END DEVICE PERFORMANCE

Target real-world compatibility with entry-level Android phones.

The app must remain usable on devices with:

- 2 GB RAM.
- 3 GB RAM.
- 4 GB RAM.
- Older supported Android versions.

The current project already contains a low-RAM detection approach. Audit it and verify that the behavior actually changes expensive rendering and animation work.

## 8.1 Adaptive performance

Create three internal performance profiles:

**Low**
- Disable expensive blur.
- Use simple translucent colors.
- Disable decorative background effects.
- Use short or reduced animations.
- Reduce image decoding size.
- Keep PDF page rendering conservative.

**Balanced**
- Moderate glass effects.
- Standard transitions.
- Normal thumbnail generation.
- Controlled PDF rendering.

**High**
- Richer visual effects when supported.
- Higher-quality previews.
- Normal animation behavior.

Select the profile automatically based on device capability.

Allow users to select a lower-performance mode in Settings if useful.

Do not promise that every device supports every effect.

## 8.2 Memory management

Implement and verify:

- Bitmap recycling or release where appropriate.
- Bounded image caches.
- Bounded PDF page caches.
- Lazy rendering.
- Thumbnail downsampling.
- Lifecycle-aware work.
- Cancellation of abandoned jobs.
- Background processing for large operations.
- No unnecessary main-thread disk I/O.

Avoid loading an entire large PDF into memory.

## 8.3 Smooth scrolling

Check all screens for:

- Excessive recompositions.
- Unstable list keys.
- Large images decoded at full resolution.
- Heavy synchronous sorting.
- Repeated database queries.
- Expensive blur in scrolling lists.
- Unnecessary state collection.
- Long-running work on the main thread.

Use LazyColumn and LazyVerticalGrid appropriately.

